{
  "summary": "Ignores the `addrMe` address received in `version` messages from privacy network peers like Tor and I2P. This prevents nodes from recording or self-advertising a peer-provided routable IP address over an anonymous connection.",
  "problem": "When connecting out to privacy network peers, those peers should not know our IP address, yet a malicious Tor or I2P peer can report an arbitrary routable IP in the `addrMe` field of their `version` message. If processed, the local node may self-advertise that address paired with its custom listening port, creating an identity fingerprint that correlates its clearnet and privacy network activity.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: 'this fixes a fingerprint which makes it possible to correlate clearnet and privacy network IPs but it requires some not so easy preconditions: 1. we need to choose this adversarial tor/i2p peer to make an outbound connection to from our addrman 2. we need to be using a unique enough port.'",
    "Commit fb178b2374: 'if we don't ignore it, we can end up sending them as self advertisement the routable ipv4 address they sent us and our listening port. ignoring it will help prevent our custom clearnet port leaks to privacy networks.'"
  ],
